{"name":"Uniform Brown","hex":"#6e5d3e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"uniform","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Uniform of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unvarying; all the same.","Consistent; conforming to one standard."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/uniform"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Uniform","blocks":[{"text":"A uniform is a variety of costume worn by members of an organization while usually participating in that organization's activity. Modern uniforms are most often worn by armed forces and paramilitary organizations such as police, emergency services, security guards, in some workplaces and schools, and by inmates in prisons. In some countries, some other officials also wear uniforms in their duties; such is the case of the Commissioned Corps of the United States Public Health Service or the French prefects. For some organizations, such as police, it may be illegal for non-members to wear the uniform."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg/330px-The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniform"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_sou